Clauses
  DISTINCT - clause is used to avoid duplicate records when doing a query, returning only unique values.
    Ex. SELECT DISTINCT name FROM books;

  GROUP BY - clause aggregates data from multiple records and groups the query results in columns
    Ex. Select name, COUNT(*) FROM books GROUP BY name;

  ORDER BY - he ORDER BY clause can be used to sort the records returned by a query in ascending or descending order. Using functions like ASC and DESC
    Ex. Select * FROM books ORDER BY (table) ASC;

  HAVING - lause is used with other clauses to filter groups or results of records based on a condition. In the case of GROUP BY, it evaluates the condition to TRUE or FALSE, unlike the WHERE clause HAVING filters the results after the aggregation is performed.
    Ex. SELECT name, COUNT(*) FROM books GROUP BY name HAVING name LIKE '%HACK%';

  Q: Using the tools_db database, what is the total number of distinct categories in the hacking_tools table?
    Command Used: SELECT DISTINCT category from hacking_tools;
    A: 6

  Q: Using the tools_db database, what is the first tool (by name) in ascending order from the hacking_tools table
    Command Used: SELECT name FROM hacking_tools ORDER BY name ASC;
    A: Bash Bunny

  Q: Using the tools_db database, what is the first tool (by name) in descending order from the hacking_tools table?
    Command Used: SELECT name FROM hacking_tools ORDER BY name DESC;
    A: Wi-Fi Pineapple

Operators
  Logical Operations
    truth coindition and return boolean values (true or false)

  Like Operator
    LIKE - operator is commonly used in conjuction with clauses
    WHERE - order to filter for specific patterns within a column
    ex. SELECT * FROM books WHERE description LIKE "%guide%";

  AND Operator
    AND operators use mutiple condiitons within a query and reutnr true if all of them are true
    ex. SELECT * FROM books WHERE cateogry = "Offensive Security" AND name = "Bug Bounty Bootcamp"

  OR Operator
    OR Operator combines mutiple conditions within queries and returns TRUE if at least one of these conditions is true
      ex. SELECT * FROM books WHERE name LIKE "%Andriod%" OR name LIKE "%iOS%";

  NOT Operator
    reverses the value of the boolean operator allowing the excldued a specific condition
      ex. SELECT * FROM books WHERE NOT descirption LIKE "%guide%";

  BETWEEN Operator
    allows for the value to exists withing a defined range
      ex. SELECT * FROM books WHERE id Between 2 AND 4;

  Comparision Operators
    compares values and checks if they meet speicifc criteria

  Equal To Operator
    = operator compares two expressions and determines if they are requal or it can check if the value matches another one in a specific column
      ex. SELECT * FROM books WHERE name = "Design Secure Software";

  Not Equal to Operator
    != compares epxressions and tests if they are not equal
      ex. SELECT * FROM books WHERE category != "Offensive Security";

  Less Than Operator
    < compares if the expression with a given value is lesser than the provided one
      ex. SELECT * FROM books WHERE published_date < "2020-01-01";

  Greater than Operator
    > Compares if the expression with a given value is greater than the provided one
      ex. SELECT * FROM books WHERE published_date > "2020-01-01";

  Less Than and Greater Than
    <= - Less than or equal
      ex. SELECT * FROM books WHERE published_date <= "2020-01-01";
    >= - Greater than Equal
      ex. SELECT * FROM books WHERE published_date =< "2020-01-01";


